{"content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,mathtools}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{longtable}\n\\usepackage[dvipsnames]{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[most]{tcolorbox}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage{listings}\n\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}\n\\usepackage[nameinlink]{cleveref}\n\n\\allowdisplaybreaks\n\\setlength{\\parskip}{0.4em}\n\\lstset{basicstyle=\\ttfamily\\small, breaklines=true, columns=fullflexible}\n\n\\newcommand{\\Z}{\\mathbb{Z}}\n\\newcommand{\\Lcal}{\\mathcal{L}}\n\\newcommand{\\KL}[2]{D\\!\\left(#1\\,\\middle\\|\\,#2\\right)}\n\\DeclareMathOperator{\\tr}{tr}\n\n\\newtcolorbox{findingbox}[1]{%\n  enhanced, sharp corners, boxrule=0.6pt,\n  colback=Green!4, colframe=Green!45!black,\n  coltitle=black, fonttitle=\\bfseries, title={#1},\n  left=8pt, right=8pt, top=6pt, bottom=6pt,\n  before skip=8pt, after skip=8pt}\n\n\\theoremstyle{remark}\n\\newtheorem*{remark}{Remark}\n\n\\title{Finite Spectral Shadows for the Collatz Valuation Cocycle:\\\\\n\\large Computational Companion}\n\\author{John A. Janik\\\\\\texttt{john.janik@gmail.com}}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis companion documents the C numerical suite \\texttt{collatz\\_tests} and the\nnine experiments (A--I) supporting the main paper, \\emph{Finite Spectral Shadows\nfor the Collatz Valuation Cocycle}. It reports the suite architecture, the exact\nconstruction of the finite residue--height operators and their Arnoldi spectral\nsolver, the per-experiment pre-registrations and results, and a dedicated\nanalysis relating the empirically detected ``shadow'' windows to the operator\ngaps. All figures are reproducible from the recorded runs. Section and equation\nreferences of the form ``main, \\S X'' point to the main paper. Throughout, the\ngoal is diagnostic: to test whether the deterministic defect cocycle\n$W_{n,\\ell}(x;\\chi,s)$ is the right finite detector and where it sees structure.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\tableofcontents\n\n\\section{The suite}\n\n\\texttt{collatz\\_tests} is an ISO~C11 program over the accelerated odd step\n$S(x)=(3x+1)/2^{b}$, $b=v_2(3x+1)$. Build profiles:\n\\textsf{FAST128} (\\texttt{\\_\\_uint128\\_t}, overflow-flagged),\n\\textsf{GMP} (\\texttt{mpz\\_t}; large seeds, exact congruence checks),\n\\textsf{DEBUG} (invariant assertions: $3x+1=2^bx'$, odd output, $b\\ge1$).\nModules: \\texttt{bigval} (the two backends), \\texttt{orbit\\_scan} (streaming),\n\\texttt{modarith}, \\texttt{characters} (additive $\\chi_k(a)=e(ka/Q)$ and\nmultiplicative on $(\\Z/3^r)^\\times$), \\texttt{defect\\_sums} ($W_{n,\\ell}$ via\ncomplex prefix sums), \\texttt{entropy\\_stats}, \\texttt{critical\\_events}\n(excursions, segment labels), \\texttt{residue\\_height\\_graph} (operator builder),\n\\texttt{sparse\\_matrix} (CSR + Arnoldi), \\texttt{io\\_csv}, \\texttt{config}.\n\n\\paragraph{Design requirements (adopted from review).}\n(1) the $\\bmod 3^r$ congruence (main, Lemma~1) is a correctness \\emph{gate}\n(Exp.~H), verified before any spectral code relies on it; (2) annealed and exact\nspectral radii are reported in \\emph{separate} columns and never substituted\n(Exp.~F); (3) the shadow search reports per-window magnitude \\emph{and} length so\nboth $\\limsup$ plateaus and long sparse blocks are visible (Exp.~I); (4) the\nquenched pressure is computed as $\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\sum s\\Delta_{b_i}$, not an\nill-defined sum over words.\n\n\\paragraph{Provisional criticality.} The project-internal ``$C_A$-critical''\ncertificate is not fixed; the suite offers \\texttt{--crit-mode} with\n\\texttt{vsq} (the literal $V^2\\le2^AP$, which is degenerate) and \\texttt{height}\n(peak $\\ge$ launch$\\cdot2^A$, the default). \\textbf{All conclusions tagged\n\\texttt{critical\\_C\\_A}, \\texttt{high\\_ascent}, \\texttt{post\\_exit} (Exp.\\ B, D,\nI) are exploratory until that certificate is fixed.}\n\n\\section{Operators and the spectral solver}\n\nThe finite operator $\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$ acts on states $(a,z)$, $a\\in U_Q$,\n$z\\in\\{0,\\dots,L\\}$, with the source-twist convention (main, \\cref*{}). The\nresidue map is forward, $a'=(3a+1)2^{-b}\\bmod Q$ for odd $Q$ ($2$ invertible),\nand inverse-branch $a'=3^{-1}(2^ba-1)$ for $Q$ coprime to $3$; the height updates\n$z'=z+\\lfloor R(\\log_2 3-b)\\rceil$ with killing off $[0,L]$.\n\n\\paragraph{Height coupling.} \\texttt{--height-mode sync} (default, faithful) uses\none valuation $b$ for both residue and height; \\texttt{factored} builds\n$\\Lcal_{\\mathrm{res}}(\\chi)\\otimes T_{\\mathrm{height}}$ with an independent height\nvaluation. The main paper shows \\texttt{sync} is the correct vehicle and\n\\texttt{factored} is a diagnostic only.\n\n\\paragraph{Arnoldi solver.} Power iteration fails on the killed-height operator\n(a near-degenerate top cluster from the height-transport mode: residuals\n$3$--$9\\%$, and spurious gap ratios $>1$ violating Wielandt). It is replaced by a\nrestarted complex Arnoldi: complex Givens rotations, a Hessenberg single-shift QR\neigensolver (Wilkinson shift, deflation), modified Gram--Schmidt with one\nreorthogonalisation, Krylov dimension grown to convergence. Validated against\nknown spectra (real/complex diagonal, cyclic permutation = roots of unity,\nagreement with power iteration). All operator runs then converge to residual\n$<8\\times10^{-11}$ with no Wielandt violations.\n\n\\section{Experiments A--I}\n\n\\subsection*{H --- congruence gate}\nVerifies main, Lemma~1.\n\\begin{findingbox}{Verified}\n$23{,}905{,}968/23{,}905{,}968$ checks match (FAST128, seeds $3$--$10^6$, $r\\le8$,\n$0$ overflow) and $144/144$ under GMP for a $16$-digit seed at $r=12$, $n\\le155$\n(values $\\gg2^{128}$).\n\\end{findingbox}\n\n\\subsection*{A --- height-tail \\& critical census}\nPre-registered: $\\log_2\\Pr(h\\ge H)\\approx-H+C$. Octave $24$, $10^5$ seeds: the\nper-step slope over $H=3,\\dots,11$ averages $\\approx-0.97$. \\textbf{Confirmed.}\n\n\\subsection*{B --- empirical Cram\\'er-tilt law}\nPre-registered: critical-excursion bulk $\\to p^{(1)}_b=3\\cdot4^{-b}$,\nentropy/digit $\\to H_2(3/4)=0.8113$.\n\\begin{findingbox}{Direction confirmed (provisional labels)}\nOctave $26$, $2\\times10^5$ seeds, boundary-excluded \\emph{middle} segments\n($1.0\\times10^5$ segs): $\\hat p=(0.657,0.229,0.089,0.021)$,\n$\\KL{\\hat p}{p^{(1)}}=0.093\\ll\\KL{\\hat p}{p}=0.191$, entropy/digit $0.870$.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\n\\subsection*{C --- defect windows}\n$|W|$ at $s=0$ decreases with window length ($0.31$ at $L{=}16\\to0.11$ at\n$L{\\approx}116$, $Q=27$). Single-orbit windows are short (orbits terminate), so\nthe asymptotic decorrelation is read from the operators (E/F), not single orbits.\n\n\\subsection*{D --- post-exit taboo (provisional)}\n$6{,}496$ post-exit windows: $\\KL{\\hat p_{\\rm post}}{p}=0.177\\ll\n\\KL{\\hat p_{\\rm post}}{p^{(1)}}=0.576$, mean $M_{\\rm post}(1)=0.989<1$. Post-exit\nrelaxes toward annealed, away from the critical tilt. (Conditional on the\nworking criticality definition.)\n\n\\subsection*{E/F --- finite operator gaps}\n\\begin{findingbox}{Gaps present; exact $\\ne$ annealed; constant by conductor}\nResidue-only: every nontrivial sector gapped; the $\\chi_2$ ratio equals the\nclosed form $\\tfrac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}$ (main, Prop.) to the digit. Exact vs\nannealed Fourier (48 sectors): $\\mathrm{corr}=0.38$, mean deviation $0.25$---the\nexact gap is \\emph{not} the i.i.d.\\ value. On the faithful \\texttt{sync} operator\nafter the coboundary quotient, the gap ratio is $r$-independent and organised by\nconductor (main, Prop.\\ on autonomy):\n\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{c|ccccc}\n\\toprule\nconductor & $3\\,(\\chi_2)$ & $9$ & $27$ & $81$ & $243$\\\\\norder & $2$ & $6$ & $9$ & $54$ & $81$\\\\\ngap ratio ($s{=}0$) & $1.0000$ & $\\mathbf{0.9069}$ & $0.8530$ & $0.8261$ & $0.7306$\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}\neach entry equal across $Q\\in\\{9,27,81,243\\}$; worst non-coboundary sector at the\nfixed conductor $9$, $\\kappa_0\\approx0.0931$.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\n\\subsection*{G --- trace/determinant shadows}\nSmall $(Q,L)$: $\\tr(\\Lcal^1)=0$, $\\tr(\\Lcal^2)=1.25$, $\\tr(\\Lcal^4)=0.594$;\n$\\det(1-z\\Lcal)$ smooth and positive---no spurious peripheral structure.\n\n\\subsection*{I --- quenched spectral-shadow search (provisional labels)}\nShadow flag: $\\max_{Q,\\chi,s}|W|\\ge\\delta$ \\emph{and} $\\ge3\\times$ a length-scaled\nnull. Over $1{,}254{,}232$ windows, $4.2\\%$ flag; by label\n$\\texttt{critical\\_C\\_A }8.4\\%>\\texttt{high\\_ascent }5.4\\%>\\texttt{post\\_exit }3.2\\%$;\nshadow rate grows with length to $100\\%$ at length $\\ge64$ ($n=218$).\n\n\\section{Relating shadows to operator gaps}\n\nWe join the flagged shadow windows (moduli $\\{9,27,81,243\\}$, all powers of $3$,\nmultiplicative characters) to the residue-only operator gaps (Exp.~F) on\n$(Q,\\chi\\text{-index},s)$.\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Tilt-level correspondence (clean); sector-level confounded}\nOf $53{,}158$ shadow windows, $82\\%$ (and $100\\%$ of the $218$ long ones) sit at\nthe critical tilt $s=1$, where F's gaps are weakest; long shadows sit in\nweaker-gap sectors (mean gap ratio $0.73$ vs.\\ $0.57$). But\n$\\mathrm{corr}(|W|,\\text{gap})=+0.25$ only: $94\\%$ of shadows pick the smallest\nmodulus $Q=9$ (six unit residues), so the per-sector signal is dominated by\nfinite residue-class inflation, not the asymptotic gap.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{Interpretation.} The finite operator decorrelates every\nnon-coboundary sector; the long shadows track it at the tilt level and in gap\nmagnitude, but the per-orbit signal is confounded by small-$Q$ finite size---the\ncircularity leak made explicit. This reinforces that the operator gap (not the\nsingle-orbit Weyl sum) is the reliable object, and that the faithful \\texttt{sync}\noperator on the coboundary quotient is the right vehicle (main, \\S Refined target).\n\n\\section{Reproducibility}\n\nEvery output is append-only CSV with a provenance header (git commit, compiler,\nbuild profile, command line, UTC timestamp). The large-scale sweep is regenerated\nby a single driver script; the operator runs use the Arnoldi solver above. AI\nprovenance for the development is tracked with \\texttt{ailog}.\n\n\\end{document}\n","file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/finite_spectral_shadows_computational.tex"}